WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.280
Welcome back.

2
00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:06.960
In this video, we will discuss ecommerce analytics.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.840
So far, you have built your data model in Power BI, created metrics for customers

4
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.360
and orders, and understood the business goals, such as cross-selling and upselling.

5
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:23.000
Whiskique's management wants you to dive deep into quantity upsell strategies.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.600
This particular strategy increases sales and saves on shipping costs.

7
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:35.120
You aim to build dashboard-style pages to present shipping costs by region and product.

8
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.880
You will also build a what-if analysis dashboard-style page to present

9
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:46.000
the impact of shipping higher product quantities on sales and profitability.

10
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.080
Whiskique hasn't automated integration with shipping

11
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.800
providers and does not capture shipping costs at the transaction level.

12
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.440
Monitoring shipping costs remain a focus for the management.

13
00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:05.960
Let us look at a fictitious example of shipping a glass jar of spices from California to Texas.

14
00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.240
The cost of shipping an individual jar is $10.

15
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:17.760
Suppose you were to ship a crate with nine spices; the package dimensions and the total weight increase.

16
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:24.800
The shipment now costs $45 or $5 per jar for nine jars.

17
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.080
The per unit cost went down as the quantity increased.

18
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:37.160
These savings can be passed on to the business as extra profit or to the customers as extra savings.

19
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:43.080
Besides, this is great for the environment — a win-win solution for everyone.

20
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:49.080
Whiskique's management wants to implement a similar strategy for their pet food business.

21
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:54.200
In a what-if analysis, you empower users to visualize the impact

22
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:59.840
of changing parameter values—for example, average shipped quantity.

23
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:06.240
Users can see the impact on business operations when combined with interactive filters.

24
00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.280
The management can view the shipping cost savings at the individual product level and

25
00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:18.480
across the entire business using the what-if analysis dashboard-style page you will build.

26
00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.600
As you build your dashboard-style pages, the following data visualization

27
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:28.200
guidelines will help you structure your pages and effectively communicate insights.

28
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:35.360
Add appropriate page and chart titles that answer the goal of a dashboard and that of each chart.

29
00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:41.600
Display key KPIs, for example, sales and shipping cost savings, in large numbers.

30
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:50.840
And finally, Interactivity using parameters and filters improves user engagement and invokes follow-up questions.

31
00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:57.960
Let us now start building our what-if analysis dashboard-style page.

